NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throttling limits how often a function can execute while calls continue to arrive. Unlike debouncing, throttling does not wait for activity to stop. It creates a bounded execution interval that works well for continuous events and updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the default settings, the first call executes immediately. Calls received during the wait period are consolidated into one trailing execution that uses the latest arguments."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-throttling-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How throttling works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The timeline below shows a throttler that allows one execution every three ticks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"Throttling (one execution per 3 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️           ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️             ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅  ❌  ⏳  ->   ✅  ❌  ❌  ❌  ✅             ✅\n             [================================================================]\n             ^ At most one execution per interval\n\n             [First burst]    [More calls]              [Spaced calls]\n             Execute first    Keep latest trailing      Execute when allowed","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls may be discarded, but execution continues at a predictable interval while activity is ongoing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-throttling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use throttling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose throttling when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Work should continue while events are arriving."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Executions should be spaced by a minimum interval."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intermediate calls may be discarded."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Immediate feedback from the first call is useful."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose another utility when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Work should wait until activity stops. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"debouncing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A specific number of calls may run within a window. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rate limiting"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every operation must eventually run. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queuing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Several items should run together. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"batching"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async throttling"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-throttling-in-tanstack-pacer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using throttling in TanStack Pacer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Pacer provides two core throttling APIs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttle"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a throttled function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Throttler"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes lifecycle methods, dynamic options, callbacks, and observable state."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"convenience-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convenience function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttle"},{"type":"text","value":" when you only need to invoke the throttled function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { throttle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst updateScrollPosition = throttle(\n  (position: number) => {\n    renderScrollPosition(position)\n  },\n  { wait: 100 },\n)\n\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  updateScrollPosition(window.scrollY)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"class-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Class API"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Throttler"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need to inspect or control pending work:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Throttler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst scrollThrottler = new Throttler(renderScrollPosition, {\n  wait: 100,\n})\n\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(120) // Executes immediately.\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(180) // Becomes the trailing call.\n\nconsole.log(scrollThrottler.store.state.isPending) // true","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both the function returned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttle"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Throttler.maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":" return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"results-and-errors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Results and errors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The synchronous throttler does not retain return values or catch errors. A leading error propagates from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":". A trailing execution runs later from a timer, so handle its errors inside the wrapped function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async throttling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when the caller must await a result or when the utility should manage async errors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"execution-timing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execution timing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing"},{"type":"text","value":" options control which edges of the throttle interval may execute."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execute the first call immediately and the latest blocked call at the trailing edge. This is the default."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execute immediately when allowed and discard calls during the interval."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delay the first execution until the trailing edge and use the latest arguments received during the interval."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not execute any calls."}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  leading: true,\n  trailing: true,\n})\n\nthrottler.maybeExecute(10) // Executes immediately.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.maybeExecute(30) // Executes at the trailing edge with 30.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls received during an existing interval update the trailing arguments without restarting that interval. This is the central difference from debouncing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlling-pending-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlling pending work"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"flush","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flush"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" immediately executes the pending trailing call. It does nothing when no trailing call is pending."}]},{"type":"code","value":"throttler.maybeExecute(10) // Leading execution.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20) // Pending trailing execution.\nthrottler.flush() // Executes with 20 now.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" discards the pending trailing call and clears its stored arguments. It does not reset the timing of the most recent completed execution."}]},{"type":"code","value":"throttler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.cancel()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reset","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" restores state counters and timing values to their defaults. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" when pending work must be discarded."}]},{"type":"code","value":"throttler.cancel()\nthrottler.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuring-behavior-at-runtime","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuring behavior at runtime"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" to update options after construction:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"throttler.setOptions({\n  wait: 250,\n  trailing: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A changed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" value does not reschedule an existing trailing timeout. It applies to later scheduling and executions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" options may be functions that receive the throttler instance:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.executionCount < 10 ? 100 : 250,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling a throttler through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" cancels a pending trailing execution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"observing-executions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observing executions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExecute"},{"type":"text","value":" runs after the wrapped function and receives the executed arguments followed by the throttler instance:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 100,\n  onExecute: (args, throttler) => {\n    console.log('Rendered value:', args[0])\n    console.log('Executions:', throttler.store.state.executionCount)\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttlerOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The class stores its state at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttler.store"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commonly useful properties include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPending"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether a trailing execution is waiting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastArgs"},{"type":"text","value":": The arguments retained for a possible trailing execution."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":": When the wrapped function last executed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":": When another execution can occur."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": How many times the wrapped function has executed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'disabled'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'idle'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'pending'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../reference/classes/Throttler.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Throttler"},{"type":"text","value":" API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for complete option and state types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-throttling-works","text":"How throttling works","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-throttling","text":"When to use throttling","level":2},{"id":"using-throttling-in-tanstack-pacer","text":"Using throttling in TanStack Pacer","level":2},{"id":"convenience-function","text":"Convenience function","level":3},{"id":"class-api","text":"Class API","level":3},{"id":"results-and-errors","text":"Results and errors","level":3},{"id":"execution-timing","text":"Execution timing","level":2},{"id":"controlling-pending-work","text":"Controlling pending work","level":2},{"id":"flush","text":"Flush","level":3},{"id":"cancel","text":"Cancel","level":3},{"id":"reset","text":"Reset","level":3},{"id":"configuring-behavior-at-runtime","text":"Configuring behavior at runtime","level":2},{"id":"observing-executions","text":"Observing executions","level":3},{"id":"state","text":"State","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThrottling limits how often a function can execute while calls continue to arrive. Unlike debouncing, throttling does not wait for activity to stop. It creates a bounded execution interval that works well for continuous events and updates.\n\nWith the default settings, the first call executes immediately. Calls received during the wait period are consolidated into one trailing execution that uses the latest arguments.\n\n## How throttling works\n\nThe timeline below shows a throttler that allows one execution every three ticks:\n\n```text\nThrottling (one execution per 3 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️           ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️             ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅  ❌  ⏳  ->   ✅  ❌  ❌  ❌  ✅             ✅\n             [================================================================]\n             ^ At most one execution per interval\n\n             [First burst]    [More calls]              [Spaced calls]\n             Execute first    Keep latest trailing      Execute when allowed\n```\n\nCalls may be discarded, but execution continues at a predictable interval while activity is ongoing.\n\n## When to use throttling\n\nChoose throttling when:\n\n- Work should continue while events are arriving.\n- Executions should be spaced by a minimum interval.\n- Intermediate calls may be discarded.\n- Immediate feedback from the first call is useful.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Work should wait until activity stops. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- A specific number of calls may run within a window. Use [rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md).\n- Every operation must eventually run. Use [queuing](./queuing.md).\n- Several items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use [async throttling](./async-throttling.md).\n\n## Using throttling in TanStack Pacer\n\nTanStack Pacer provides two core throttling APIs:\n\n- `throttle` returns a throttled function.\n- `Throttler` exposes lifecycle methods, dynamic options, callbacks, and observable state.\n\n### Convenience function\n\nUse `throttle` when you only need to invoke the throttled function:\n\n```ts\nimport { throttle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst updateScrollPosition = throttle(\n  (position: number) => {\n    renderScrollPosition(position)\n  },\n  { wait: 100 },\n)\n\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  updateScrollPosition(window.scrollY)\n})\n```\n\n### Class API\n\nUse `Throttler` when you need to inspect or control pending work:\n\n```ts\nimport { Throttler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst scrollThrottler = new Throttler(renderScrollPosition, {\n  wait: 100,\n})\n\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(120) // Executes immediately.\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(180) // Becomes the trailing call.\n\nconsole.log(scrollThrottler.store.state.isPending) // true\n```\n\nBoth the function returned by `throttle` and `Throttler.maybeExecute()` return `void`.\n\n### Results and errors\n\nThe synchronous throttler does not retain return values or catch errors. A leading error propagates from `maybeExecute()`. A trailing execution runs later from a timer, so handle its errors inside the wrapped function.\n\nUse [async throttling](./async-throttling.md) when the caller must await a result or when the utility should manage async errors.\n\n## Execution timing\n\nThe `leading` and `trailing` options control which edges of the throttle interval may execute.\n\n| `leading` | `trailing` | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `true` | `true` | Execute the first call immediately and the latest blocked call at the trailing edge. This is the default. |\n| `true` | `false` | Execute immediately when allowed and discard calls during the interval. |\n| `false` | `true` | Delay the first execution until the trailing edge and use the latest arguments received during the interval. |\n| `false` | `false` | Do not execute any calls. |\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  leading: true,\n  trailing: true,\n})\n\nthrottler.maybeExecute(10) // Executes immediately.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.maybeExecute(30) // Executes at the trailing edge with 30.\n```\n\nCalls received during an existing interval update the trailing arguments without restarting that interval. This is the central difference from debouncing.\n\n## Controlling pending work\n\n### Flush\n\n`flush()` immediately executes the pending trailing call. It does nothing when no trailing call is pending.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.maybeExecute(10) // Leading execution.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20) // Pending trailing execution.\nthrottler.flush() // Executes with 20 now.\n```\n\n### Cancel\n\n`cancel()` discards the pending trailing call and clears its stored arguments. It does not reset the timing of the most recent completed execution.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.cancel()\n```\n\n### Reset\n\n`reset()` restores state counters and timing values to their defaults. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call `cancel()` before `reset()` when pending work must be discarded.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.cancel()\nthrottler.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuring behavior at runtime\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update options after construction:\n\n```ts\nthrottler.setOptions({\n  wait: 250,\n  trailing: false,\n})\n```\n\nA changed `wait` value does not reschedule an existing trailing timeout. It applies to later scheduling and executions.\n\nThe `enabled` and `wait` options may be functions that receive the throttler instance:\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.executionCount < 10 ? 100 : 250,\n})\n```\n\nDisabling a throttler through `setOptions()` cancels a pending trailing execution.\n\n### Observing executions\n\n`onExecute` runs after the wrapped function and receives the executed arguments followed by the throttler instance:\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 100,\n  onExecute: (args, throttler) => {\n    console.log('Rendered value:', args[0])\n    console.log('Executions:', throttler.store.state.executionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nTo share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with `throttlerOptions()`.\n\n## State\n\nThe class stores its state at `throttler.store`.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful properties include:\n\n- `isPending`: Whether a trailing execution is waiting.\n- `lastArgs`: The arguments retained for a possible trailing execution.\n- `lastExecutionTime`: When the wrapped function last executed.\n- `nextExecutionTime`: When another execution can occur.\n- `executionCount`: How many times the wrapped function has executed.\n- `status`: `'disabled'`, `'idle'`, or `'pending'`.\n\nSee the [`Throttler` API reference](../../../reference/classes/Throttler.md) for complete option and state types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Vanilla Throttling Guide\nid: throttling\n---\n\nThrottling limits how often a function can execute while calls continue to arrive. Unlike debouncing, throttling does not wait for activity to stop. It creates a bounded execution interval that works well for continuous events and updates.\n\nWith the default settings, the first call executes immediately. Calls received during the wait period are consolidated into one trailing execution that uses the latest arguments.\n\n## How throttling works\n\nThe timeline below shows a throttler that allows one execution every three ticks:\n\n```text\nThrottling (one execution per 3 ticks)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️           ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️  ⬇️             ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅  ❌  ⏳  ->   ✅  ❌  ❌  ❌  ✅             ✅\n             [================================================================]\n             ^ At most one execution per interval\n\n             [First burst]    [More calls]              [Spaced calls]\n             Execute first    Keep latest trailing      Execute when allowed\n```\n\nCalls may be discarded, but execution continues at a predictable interval while activity is ongoing.\n\n## When to use throttling\n\nChoose throttling when:\n\n- Work should continue while events are arriving.\n- Executions should be spaced by a minimum interval.\n- Intermediate calls may be discarded.\n- Immediate feedback from the first call is useful.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Work should wait until activity stops. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- A specific number of calls may run within a window. Use [rate limiting](./rate-limiting.md).\n- Every operation must eventually run. Use [queuing](./queuing.md).\n- Several items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use [async throttling](./async-throttling.md).\n\n## Using throttling in TanStack Pacer\n\nTanStack Pacer provides two core throttling APIs:\n\n- `throttle` returns a throttled function.\n- `Throttler` exposes lifecycle methods, dynamic options, callbacks, and observable state.\n\n### Convenience function\n\nUse `throttle` when you only need to invoke the throttled function:\n\n```ts\nimport { throttle } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst updateScrollPosition = throttle(\n  (position: number) => {\n    renderScrollPosition(position)\n  },\n  { wait: 100 },\n)\n\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  updateScrollPosition(window.scrollY)\n})\n```\n\n### Class API\n\nUse `Throttler` when you need to inspect or control pending work:\n\n```ts\nimport { Throttler } from '@tanstack/pacer'\n\nconst scrollThrottler = new Throttler(renderScrollPosition, {\n  wait: 100,\n})\n\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(120) // Executes immediately.\nscrollThrottler.maybeExecute(180) // Becomes the trailing call.\n\nconsole.log(scrollThrottler.store.state.isPending) // true\n```\n\nBoth the function returned by `throttle` and `Throttler.maybeExecute()` return `void`.\n\n### Results and errors\n\nThe synchronous throttler does not retain return values or catch errors. A leading error propagates from `maybeExecute()`. A trailing execution runs later from a timer, so handle its errors inside the wrapped function.\n\nUse [async throttling](./async-throttling.md) when the caller must await a result or when the utility should manage async errors.\n\n## Execution timing\n\nThe `leading` and `trailing` options control which edges of the throttle interval may execute.\n\n| `leading` | `trailing` | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `true` | `true` | Execute the first call immediately and the latest blocked call at the trailing edge. This is the default. |\n| `true` | `false` | Execute immediately when allowed and discard calls during the interval. |\n| `false` | `true` | Delay the first execution until the trailing edge and use the latest arguments received during the interval. |\n| `false` | `false` | Do not execute any calls. |\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  leading: true,\n  trailing: true,\n})\n\nthrottler.maybeExecute(10) // Executes immediately.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.maybeExecute(30) // Executes at the trailing edge with 30.\n```\n\nCalls received during an existing interval update the trailing arguments without restarting that interval. This is the central difference from debouncing.\n\n## Controlling pending work\n\n### Flush\n\n`flush()` immediately executes the pending trailing call. It does nothing when no trailing call is pending.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.maybeExecute(10) // Leading execution.\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20) // Pending trailing execution.\nthrottler.flush() // Executes with 20 now.\n```\n\n### Cancel\n\n`cancel()` discards the pending trailing call and clears its stored arguments. It does not reset the timing of the most recent completed execution.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.maybeExecute(20)\nthrottler.cancel()\n```\n\n### Reset\n\n`reset()` restores state counters and timing values to their defaults. It does not clear an already scheduled timeout. Call `cancel()` before `reset()` when pending work must be discarded.\n\n```ts\nthrottler.cancel()\nthrottler.reset()\n```\n\n## Configuring behavior at runtime\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update options after construction:\n\n```ts\nthrottler.setOptions({\n  wait: 250,\n  trailing: false,\n})\n```\n\nA changed `wait` value does not reschedule an existing trailing timeout. It applies to later scheduling and executions.\n\nThe `enabled` and `wait` options may be functions that receive the throttler instance:\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  enabled: (throttler) => throttler.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  wait: (throttler) =>\n    throttler.store.state.executionCount < 10 ? 100 : 250,\n})\n```\n\nDisabling a throttler through `setOptions()` cancels a pending trailing execution.\n\n### Observing executions\n\n`onExecute` runs after the wrapped function and receives the executed arguments followed by the throttler instance:\n\n```ts\nconst throttler = new Throttler(updateProgress, {\n  wait: 100,\n  onExecute: (args, throttler) => {\n    console.log('Rendered value:', args[0])\n    console.log('Executions:', throttler.store.state.executionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nTo share a type-checked configuration across instances, define it with `throttlerOptions()`.\n\n## State\n\nThe class stores its state at `throttler.store`.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful properties include:\n\n- `isPending`: Whether a trailing execution is waiting.\n- `lastArgs`: The arguments retained for a possible trailing execution.\n- `lastExecutionTime`: When the wrapped function last executed.\n- `nextExecutionTime`: When another execution can occur.\n- `executionCount`: How many times the wrapped function has executed.\n- `status`: `'disabled'`, `'idle'`, or `'pending'`.\n\nSee the [`Throttler` API reference](../../../reference/classes/Throttler.md) for complete option and state types.\n"}
